- `action(n1, "start the flickr login process")`: Hallucinated step; the source begins directly with "please provide the userdata for flickr." (SMC1)
- `guard(b1, n5, v1 = 'Yes')` & `guard(b3, n10, v3 = 'Yes')`: Guard conditions are inverted; routing a successful login value to a retry decision node contradicts "If the login is successful, the process will end." (SMC4, SMC5)
- `defines(v1, "is the login successful", {"Yes"})`, `defines(v3, "...", {"Yes"})`, `defines(v5, "...", {})`: Variable ranges are incomplete or empty; they must enumerate both possible outcomes (e.g., success/failure) to support the binary evaluations in the source. (SMC6)
- `defines(v2, "do you want to try again", {})`, `defines(v4, "...", {})`: Ranges are empty; must define valid options corresponding to the source's explicit "try again" vs "abort" choices. (SMC6)
- `branch(b2, XOR)`, `branch(b4, XOR)`, `branch(b5, XOR)`: Lack required `guard` statements despite modeling explicit user decisions; resulting control-flow paths are unconstrained and do not enforce documented choices. (SMC4)
- `label(n2, n3, "No")`, `label(n6, n8, "No")`, `label(n11, n13, "No")`: Labels attach outcome indicators to mandatory sequential precedence edges instead of to the outgoing edges of the corresponding `branch` nodes where the decision/failure actually occurs. (SMC2, SMC4)
- `action(n4, "end process successful login")`, `action(n9, "end process successful login after retry")`, `action(n14, "end process successful login after third try")`: Hallucinated redundant steps immediately preceding `end` nodes; the source simply states "the process will end." (SMC1)

Structural Check
- [S1] STC11 failed: Each variable definition must have a valid range with at least two distinct values. | Variable `v1` has an invalid range `['Yes']`. | Variable `v2` has an invalid range `[]`. | Variable `v3` has an invalid range `['Yes']`. | Variable `v4` has an invalid range `[]`. | Variable `v5` has an invalid range `[]`.
- [S2] STC15 failed: Every variable used in a guard must be formally defined. | Guard `v1 = 'Yes'` on `b1 -> n5` uses undefined variable `v1`. | Guard `v3 = 'Yes'` on `b3 -> n10` uses undefined variable `v3`. | DSL: guard(b1,n5,"v1 = 'Yes'"); guard(b3,n10,"v3 = 'Yes'")
- [S3] STC16 failed: Every outgoing edge of XOR/OR branches must have exactly one guard. | XOR/OR edge `b1 -> n4` has 0 guards; exactly one is required. | XOR/OR edge `b2 -> n6` has 0 guards; exactly one is required. | XOR/OR edge `b2 -> n7` has 0 guards; exactly one is required. | XOR/OR edge `b3 -> n9` has 0 guards; exactly one is required. | XOR/OR edge `b4 -> n11` has 0 guards; exactly one is required. | XOR/OR edge `b4 -> n12` has 0 guards; exactly one is required. | XOR/OR edge `b5 -> n14` has 0 guards; exactly one is required. | XOR/OR edge `b5 -> n15` has 0 guards; exactly one is required.

Logical Sanity Check
- [L1] LSC1 failed: Every guard must be satisfiable under the declared ranges of its referenced variables. | Guard `v1 = 'Yes'` on `b1 -> n5` cannot be evaluated: `v1` has no declared range. | Guard `v3 = 'Yes'` on `b3 -> n10` cannot be evaluated: `v3` has no declared range. | DSL: guard(b1,n5,"v1 = 'Yes'"); guard(b3,n10,"v3 = 'Yes'")
- [L2] LSC3 failed: Guards outgoing from XOR/OR branches must cover the whole evaluated state space. | Branch `b1` cannot be evaluated: `v1` has no declared range. | Branch `b3` cannot be evaluated: `v3` has no declared range.
